%S A401276 0,0,2,369,55988,7911975,1074780086,142277581761,18495267571496,
%T A401276 2372430414172635,301269149932393610,37961762294057710053,
%U A401276 4754441384556703007324,592598698734823452545055,73577839897385739725836574,9107137929332649525273294345,1124396256474307472786195496272
%N A401276 a(n) is the number k such that 122^n - 102^n - 21^n = 2020 * k - 1.
%C A401276 The problem 4 of the New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad, Round One in 2019 asked to "show that the number 122^n - 102^n - 21^n is always one less than a multiple of 2020, for any positive integer n". A nice proof with congruence is available in the link. This sequence proposes here the corresponding integers k (for n>=0).
%H A401276 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad Committee, <a href="https://www.mathsolympiad.org.nz/competitions/nzmo/files/problems.php?round=1&amp;year=2019&amp;type=solutions">2019, NZMO Round 1, problem 4</a>.
%H A401276 <a href="/index/O#Olympiads">Index to sequences related to Olympiads and other Mathematical Competitions</a>.
%H A401276 <a href="/index/Rec#order_04">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (246,-17393,278472,-261324).
%F A401276 a(n) = (122^n - 102^n - 21^n + 1)/2020.
%F A401276 a(n) = 246*a(n-1) - 17393*a(n-2) + 278472*a(n-3) - 261324*a(n-4) for n >= 4, with a(0) = 0, a(1) = 0, a(2) = 2, a(3) = 369.
%F A401276 From _Elmo R. Oliveira_, Oct 28 2026: (Start)
%F A401276 G.f.: x^2*(2 - 123*x) / ((1 - x)*(1 - 21*x)*(1 - 102*x)*(1 - 122*x)).
%F A401276 E.g.f.: (1/2020)*(1 - exp(20*x) - exp(101*x) + exp(121*x))*exp(x). (End)
%e A401276 122^3 - 102^3 - 21^3 = 745379 = 2020 * 369 - 1, so a(3) = 369.
%t A401276 a[n_] := (122^n - 102^n - 21^n + 1)/2020; Array[a, 17, 0] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 19 2026 *)
%o A401276 (PARI) my(x='x+O('x^15)); concat([0,0], Vec(-(123*x-2)/((x-1)*(21*x-1)*(102*x-1)*(122*x-1)))) \\ _Elmo R. Oliveira_, Oct 28 2026
%K A401276 nonn,easy,new
%O A401276 0,3
%A A401276 _Bernard Schott_, Oct 19 2026
